    "moduleId": "lld-intro",
    "subtitle": "Two pictures power every LLD lesson, the class diagram (structure) and the sequence diagram (behaviour). Learn to r…",
    "theoryMarkdown": "## Why two diagrams?\n\nLow-Level Design answers two questions about a system:\n\n- **What are the pieces, and how do they relate?** → the **class diagram** (structure).\n- **How do the pieces talk to get one job done?** → the **sequence diagram** (behaviour).\n\nMaster these two and you can read, and draw, almost any design in an interview.\n\n## The class diagram\n\nEach **class** is a box with three parts: the **name**, its **attributes** (data), and its **operations** (methods). Visibility markers: `+` public, `-` private, `#` protected.\n\nThe arrows between boxes are the whole point, they say *who depends on whom*:\n\n- **▷ solid (inheritance)**, *is-a*. `SavingsAccount` **extends** `Account`.\n- **▷ dashed (realization)**, *implements an interface*. `Account` realizes `Statement`.\n- **◆ filled diamond (composition)**, *owns, lifecycle-bound*. Destroy the `Account` and its `TransactionLog` dies with it.\n- **◇ hollow diamond (aggregation)**, *has-a, shared*. The part can outlive the whole.\n- **→ open arrow (association)**, *holds a reference to*.\n- **⇢ dashed arrow (dependency)**, *uses transiently* (a parameter, a return type).\n\n> Rule of thumb: prefer the **weakest** relationship that does the job. Composition over inheritance, dependency over composition. Looser coupling = easier change.\n\n## The sequence diagram\n\nTime flows **downward**. Each **lifeline** (the dashed vertical line) is one object's existence. A **message** is a method call drawn left-to-right:\n\n- **solid arrow**, a synchronous **call**.\n- **dashed arrow**, a **return** (the reply).\n- a **self-loop**, an object calling its own method.\n\nRead it top to bottom and you're literally reading the method calls of one use-case, in order.\n\nThat's the whole vocabulary. Hit **Watch** and see both come alive.",
