    "id": "objects",
    "patternId": "lld-foundations",
    "title": "Objects & Classes",
    "kind": "concept",
    "difficulty": "Intro",
    "statement": "A class is a blueprint; an object is a concrete instance created from it, with its own state.",
    "companies": [
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Adobe"
    ],
    "theory": "## Class, the blueprint\n\nA **class** is a *template*. It defines two things: the **fields** an object will hold (its **state**) and the **methods** that act on that state (its **behaviour**). On its own a class stores nothing, it is just a description, like architectural plans for a house. `Car` says “every car HAS a brand and a speed, and CAN accelerate and brake”, but it is not itself a car you can drive.\n\n## Object, a runtime instance\n\nAn **object** is what you get when you build from the blueprint. The `new` keyword **allocates** a fresh object in memory and hands you a reference to it:\n\n```\nCar tesla = new Car();   // a real, drivable instance\n```\n\nThat object has its **OWN copy** of every field. Writing `tesla.speed = 0` touches only `tesla`'s memory, nobody else's.\n\n## One class, many objects\n\nFrom a **single** class you can stamp out as many objects as you like, and each is **independent**. `tesla` and `mini` share the same shape and the same methods, yet `tesla.brand` and `mini.brand` live in separate slots, change one and the other is untouched.\n\n## Identity vs. state\n\nTwo objects can hold **equal state** and still be **different instances**. `new Car()` and another `new Car()` with identical fields are *not the same object*, each has its own **identity** (its own address in memory). State is *what an object holds*; identity is *which object it is*.\n\n## Compile-time vs. runtime\n\nThe class exists at **compile time**, it is written in your source and fixed before the program runs. Objects come to life at **runtime**, created and destroyed as the program executes. One static `Car` definition; potentially millions of `Car` objects flowing through memory while the app is running.",
