    "id": "inheritance",
    "patternId": "lld-foundations",
    "title": "Inheritance",
    "kind": "concept",
    "difficulty": "Intro",
    "statement": "A subclass IS-A superclass, it inherits the parent’s fields and methods for free, and may add to or override them.",
    "companies": [
      "Amazon",
      "Microsoft",
      "Oracle"
    ],
    "theory": "## The is-a relationship\n\n**Inheritance** models an **is-a** relationship: a `Motorcycle` **is a** `Vehicle`, a `Car` **is a** `Vehicle`. You write the shared idea once in a **base class** and let the specific types extend it.\n\nThe payoff is **code reuse**. Put the common fields and methods in the parent, and every child gets them **automatically**, no copy-paste:\n\n```\nclass Vehicle {\n    protected int speed;\n    void start() { ... }    // shared once\n    void stop()  { ... }\n}\n\nclass Car extends Vehicle {  // gets start/stop/speed for free\n    void openTrunk() { ... } // and ADDS its own\n}\n```\n\n## Override to specialise\n\nA child can keep an inherited method as-is, or **override** it to change the behaviour. A `Motorcycle` might override `start()` to kick-start instead of turning a key. It can even call the parent's version first with `super`:\n\n- **`super.start()`** runs the inherited logic, then the child adds its own steps, extending, not just replacing.\n\n## Single vs multiple inheritance\n\nLanguages differ on how many parents a class may have. **Java** allows **single** class inheritance (one `extends`) but **multiple interface** inheritance (many `implements`). C++ allows multiple class inheritance, at the cost of the \"diamond problem\".\n\n> Caution: reach for inheritance only for a **true, stable is-a**. If you just want to **reuse behaviour**, prefer **composition over inheritance**, hold a collaborator instead of extending it. A deep, leaky class hierarchy is far harder to change than a few small parts wired together.",
