    "moduleId": "lld-foundations",
    "subtitle": "The arrows ARE the design. Dependency, association, aggregation, composition and inheritance say exactly how tightl…",
    "theoryMarkdown": "## The lines carry the meaning\n\nTwo boxes joined by a plain line tell you almost nothing. The **kind** of line, and **which end** its decoration touches, is where the design actually lives. Read a class diagram and the arrows tell you, at a glance, *how coupled* the system is and *what breaks when something changes*.\n\nThink of relationships as a spectrum of **commitment**, from a one-night handshake to a marriage:\n\n| Relationship | Symbol | Plain English | \"Has-a\"? | Lifecycle |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Dependency | ⇢ dashed | \"uses for a moment\" | no | unrelated |\n| Association | → | \"knows / refers to\" | a reference | independent |\n| Aggregation | ◇ hollow ◆ | \"has-a, shared\" | yes | part outlives whole |\n| Composition | ◆ filled | \"owns\" | yes | part dies with whole |\n| Inheritance | ▷ triangle | \"is-a\" |, | a *type*, not ownership |\n\nThe one question that separates the \"has-a\" trio: **if I destroy the whole, what happens to the part?**\n\n## ⇢ Dependency: \"uses transiently\"\n\nThe weakest link. A only **mentions** B, as a method **parameter**, a local variable, or a return type. Nothing is stored.\n\n*Analogy:* you use a **taxi** to get to the airport. You don't own it; once you arrive, the relationship is over.\n\n```\nclass Order {\n    Money total(TaxService tax) {   // tax is just borrowed for the call\n        return subtotal + tax.on(subtotal);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n*Spot it in code:* B appears only inside a method signature — never as a field.\n\n## → Association: \"holds a reference\"\n\nA keeps a **long-lived reference** to B as a **field**, but neither created nor owns the other. They're peers.\n\n*Analogy:* a **driver and a car** — the driver has a car they drive for years, but the car was bought elsewhere and could be sold without the driver disappearing.\n\n```\nclass Driver { Car car; }   // a stored, long-lived link\n```\n\n## ◇ Aggregation vs ◆ Composition, the one everyone confuses\n\nBoth are **\"has-a\"**. Both are drawn with a diamond on the **whole** (owner) end. The *only* difference is **who controls the part's lifecycle**, and that's exactly what interviewers probe.\n\n- **◇ Aggregation (hollow diamond), shared, the part outlives the whole.**\n  *Analogy:* a **team and its players**. Disband the team and the players keep playing — they can join another team. The team didn't *create* them and doesn't *destroy* them.\n  ```\n  class Team { List<Player> roster; }   // players passed in from outside\n  ```\n\n- **◆ Composition (filled diamond), owned, the part dies with the whole.**\n  *Analogy:* a **house and its rooms**. The house *creates* its rooms, and when you demolish the house the rooms are gone. A room can't belong to two houses.\n  ```\n  class House { House(){ rooms = new Room[n]; } }   // house makes & owns them\n  ```\n\nThe litmus test: **\"can the part exist on its own, or be shared?\"** Yes → aggregation. No, it's created-and-destroyed by the owner → composition.\n\n## ▷ Inheritance, a different axis\n\n`SavingsAccount` **is-a** `Account`. This is about **type and substitutability**, not ownership, so it isn't on the strength scale at all. (It's covered in the Inheritance lesson.) The hollow triangle points at the parent.\n\n## The rule of thumb\n\n> **Prefer the weakest relationship that does the job:** dependency < association < aggregation < composition. Weaker binding = looser coupling = a design you can change without a domino effect. Reach for composition only when one object genuinely *owns* another's entire life.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Drawing **composition** when the part is actually shared or injected → it's aggregation.\n- Storing a class as a field \"just in case\" when a method parameter (dependency) would do → needless coupling.\n- Treating inheritance as a *has-a* shortcut for reuse → prefer composition (see \"Composition over Inheritance\").",
