    "theoryMarkdown": "## Two ways to share a shape\n\nBoth let you program against a type without knowing the concrete class, but they answer different questions.\n\n## Abstract class, a half-built base\n\nAn **abstract class** cannot be instantiated (`new Game()` won't compile). It is a *partial* class meant to be **extended**. It can carry:\n\n- **fields / state**, e.g. `# players`, shared by every subclass.\n- **constructors**, to initialise that state.\n- **both** concrete methods (real code subclasses inherit) **and** abstract methods (signatures subclasses must fill in).\n\n```\nabstract class Game {\n    protected int players;\n    public final void play() {      // concrete: shared template\n        initialize();\n        loop();\n        endGame();\n    }\n    protected abstract void initialize();   // subclass fills in\n    protected abstract void endGame();\n}\n```\n\nReach for it when subtypes are genuinely an **\"is-a\"** of one another and you want to **hand them shared code + state**.\n\n## Interface, a pure contract\n\nAn **interface** is a **\"can-do\"** capability: a list of method signatures with **no instance state**. Every method is abstract (modern languages allow `default` bodies, but still no fields). A class declares it **implements** the interface and supplies the bodies.\n\nThe killer feature: a class can implement **many** interfaces, so the same type can gain **unrelated** capabilities, `Savable`, `Rankable`, `Printable`without forcing them into one family tree.\n\n## The rule that decides it\n\n- You can **extend exactly ONE** abstract class (single inheritance).\n- You can **implement MANY** interfaces.\n\n## Comparison\n\n- **Instantiate**, abstract: no · interface: no\n- **Instance state / fields**, abstract: yes · interface: no\n- **Constructor**, abstract: yes · interface: no\n- **Concrete method bodies**, abstract: yes · interface: defaults only\n- **How many per class**, abstract: one · interface: many\n- **Models**, abstract: *is-a* (identity) · interface: *can-do* (capability)\n\n> Decision guide: need **shared code/state plus one identity**? → **abstract class**. Need a **capability mixable across unrelated classes**? → **interface**. When in doubt, prefer the interface, it couples less.",
