    "theoryMarkdown": "## Open for extension, closed for modification\n\nThe **O** in SOLID. A module should let you add new behaviour **without editing the code that already works**. You extend by **writing new code**, not by reopening tested code and risking the bugs that come with it.\n\n## The smell: `if/else` on a type\n\nThe tell-tale sign is a method that switches on *what kind of thing* it has:\n\n```\ndouble area(Shape s){\n    if      (s is Circle) return PI*r*r;\n    else if (s is Rect)   return w*h;\n    // add Triangle? EDIT here  ← reopen tested code\n}\n```\n\nEvery new shape forces you back into this **one** method. You touch code that already passed its tests, re-review it, re-deploy it, and the bug surface grows with each `else if`. The class is **closed to nothing**; it's reopened constantly.\n\n## Invert it with an abstraction\n\nHide the variation behind an **interface**. Each kind of behaviour becomes its **own class** that the existing code never names:\n\n```\ninterface Shape { double area(); }\ndouble total(List<Shape> shapes){\n    double t = 0;\n    for (Shape s : shapes) t += s.area();   // never says \"Circle\"\n    return t;\n}\n```\n\nA new `Triangle` is a **new class** implementing `Shape`. `AreaCalculator` never sees it and never changes, it's **closed** to modification yet **open** to extension.\n\n> This rests on **polymorphism** (one call, many forms) and on **LSP**, every `Shape` must be substitutable. It's also the same inversion as **DIP**: the high-level calculator depends on the abstraction, not the concrete shapes.",
