    "id": "dependency-inversion",
    "patternId": "lld-solid",
    "title": "Dependency Inversion (D)",
    "kind": "concept",
    "difficulty": "Core",
    "statement": "High-level policy shouldn’t depend on low-level details. Point BOTH at an abstraction, and watch the dependency arrow flip.",
    "companies": [
      "Amazon",
      "Netflix",
      "Uber",
      "Microsoft"
    ],
    "theory": "## Two rules\n\nThe **D** in SOLID:\n\n1. **High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules.** Both should depend on **abstractions**.\n2. **Abstractions should not depend on details.** Details should depend on abstractions.\n\n“High-level” = the policy/business logic (`NotificationService`). “Low-level” = the mechanism (`EmailSender`, a DB, an HTTP client).\n\n## The problem with depending on a detail\n\n```\nclass NotificationService {\n    EmailSender sender = new EmailSender();   // ← welded to email\n    void notify(String m){ sender.sendEmail(m); }\n}\n```\n\nYour core logic now **knows about email**. Want SMS? You edit the high-level class. Want a fake sender for a unit test? You can’t. The important module is hostage to a trivial one.\n\n## Invert it\n\nPut an **abstraction** (`Sender`) between them. The high-level module depends on the interface; the low-level classes **implement** it:\n\n```\nclass NotificationService {\n    Sender sender;                       // an abstraction\n    NotificationService(Sender s){ sender = s; }   // injected\n    void notify(String m){ sender.send(m); }\n}\n```\n\nThe source-code dependency from `EmailSender` now points **UP** at the abstraction, it *inverted*. That’s the name. `NotificationService` no longer knows email exists; pass it an `SmsSender`, a `MockSender`, anything.\n\n> This is the backbone of **dependency injection** and testable architecture. The interface is usually owned by the high-level module, the detail conforms to the policy, not the other way around.",
