    "theory": "## Two forces that shape every design\n\n**Cohesion** measures how *focused* a single class is: do all its methods and fields serve **one clear purpose**? **Coupling** measures how much a class *knows about* other classes — how many concrete things it reaches out and touches.\n\nThe whole goal, in four words:\n\n> **High cohesion, loose coupling.**\n\n## Low cohesion, the grab-bag\n\nA class with low cohesion is a junk drawer. The classic smell is a `Utils` / `Helper` / `Manager` class:\n\n```\nclass Utils {\n    parseDate()    sendEmail()\n    calcTax()      resizeImg()   // ← nothing in common\n}\n```\n\nThese methods have **nothing to do with each other**. Symptoms of low cohesion:\n\n- vague names like `Utils`, `Common`, `Misc`, `Manager`\n- methods that share no fields and never call each other\n- \"where do I put this?\" → it lands here by default\n\nThe fix is **SRP**: split the grab-bag into small, single-purpose classes. Each focused class is **highly cohesive**.\n\n## High coupling, the tangled web\n\nCoupling is high when classes depend **directly** on each other's concrete details. Change one, and ten break. Symptoms:\n\n- a change here forces edits in classes far away\n- you can't unit-test one class without wiring up five others\n- a dependency graph that looks like spaghetti\n\n## Loosen it with abstractions\n\nPoint dependencies at an **interface** instead of a concrete class, `Billing` talks to a `PaymentGw` abstraction, not to `Stripe` directly. Now you can swap, mock, and change implementations freely.\n\nNotice the symmetry: **SRP raises cohesion**, and **interfaces + DIP lower coupling**. Cohesion and coupling are the master dial, every other principle is just a way of turning it the right direction.",
