    "id": "law-of-demeter",
    "patternId": "lld-principles",
    "title": "Law of Demeter",
    "kind": "concept",
    "difficulty": "Core",
    "statement": "Only talk to your immediate friends. A method should call methods on itself, its parameters, objects it creates, and its direct fields, not reach through a chain of strangers.",
    "companies": [
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Shopify"
    ],
    "theory": "## Don't talk to strangers\n\nThe **Law of Demeter** (LoD), also called the **principle of least knowledge**, says an object should make assumptions about as little of the system as possible. Put plainly: *only talk to your immediate friends.*\n\nThe classic violation is the **train wreck**:\n\n```\na.getB().getC().doIt();   // reaching through strangers\n```\n\nEach `.get` digs one level deeper into a stranger's internals. The caller is now coupled to the **entire object graph** behind `a`change `C`, or how `B` exposes it, and this line breaks even though it never mentioned them by name.\n\n## The four kinds of friends\n\nA method `m` of an object `O` may only send messages to:\n\n- **`this`**, the object itself.\n- its **parameters**, the arguments handed to `m`.\n- objects it **creates** inside `m`things it `new`s up.\n- `O`'s **direct components**, its own fields / immediate collaborators.\n\nAnything else is a *stranger's stranger*, off-limits.\n\n## The fix: Tell, Don't Ask\n\nDon't **ask** an object for its internals and then act on them, **tell** the object what you want and let it do the work, because it owns the data:\n\n```\norder.pay(amt);   // not order.getCustomer().getWallet().deduct(amt)\n```\n\nPush the behaviour to the object that owns the data. Knowledge stays where the data lives, and coupling collapses to a single hop.\n\n> **Caveat:** fluent **builders** and **streams** (`builder().a().b().build()`, `list.stream().filter().map()`) chain calls on purpose, each call returns the *same* object, not a new stranger. That deliberate pattern is **not** a Demeter violation.",
