    "moduleId": "lld-structural",
    "subtitle": "Add behaviour to an object by WRAPPING it in another object of the same type, stack as many layers as you like, at …",
    "theoryMarkdown": "## Add-ons without a class explosion\n\nYou sell coffee. Customers want espresso, espresso + milk, espresso + milk + sugar, … If you make a subclass per combination you get `EspressoWithMilkAndSugar`and the count explodes with every new add-on.\n\n## Wrap instead of subclass\n\nA **Decorator** is an object that **implements the same interface** as the thing it decorates AND **holds a reference** to one. It forwards the call to the wrapped object and adds its own bit:\n\n```\nBeverage drink = new Sugar(new Milk(new Espresso()));\ndrink.cost();   // 2.75\n```\n\nEach layer is transparent, to the client it's just a `Beverage`. Calls pass **inward** through the wrappers to the core, and results bubble **outward**, each decorator adding its part.\n\n## Why it's powerful\n\n- **Compose at runtime**, stack add-ons in any order, any number.\n- **Open/Closed**, a new `Cream` decorator adds behaviour without touching existing classes.\n- Single Responsibility, each decorator does one thing.\n\n> The catch: many small wrapper objects, and `new Sugar(new Milk(new Espresso()))` can get noisy (a Builder or factory helps). Java I/O streams (`new BufferedReader(new FileReader(...))`) are the textbook real-world Decorator.",
