    "id": "composite",
    "patternId": "lld-structural",
    "title": "Composite",
    "kind": "pattern",
    "difficulty": "Core",
    "statement": "Build a tree of part-and-whole objects, then treat a single leaf and a whole branch THE SAME way through one interface.",
    "companies": [
      "Amazon",
      "Google",
      "Figma"
    ],
    "theory": "## Part-whole trees\n\nA file system is files and folders. A folder contains files **and other folders**, to any depth. A UI is widgets containing widgets. Whenever you have a **tree of part-and-whole**, Composite fits.\n\n## One interface for leaf and branch\n\nDefine a **component** interface every node shares. A **leaf** (`File`) implements it directly. A **composite** (`Folder`) implements it too, and **holds a list of components** as children. The crucial move: the composite's children are typed as the *interface*, so a folder can hold files and folders alike.\n\n```\ninterface FileItem { int size(); }\nclass File   implements FileItem { int size(){ return bytes; } }\nclass Folder implements FileItem {\n    List<FileItem> children;\n    int size(){ return children.stream().mapToInt(FileItem::size).sum(); }  // recurse\n}\n```\n\n## Uniform treatment is the payoff\n\nThe client calls `item.size()` without ever asking *\"are you a file or a folder?\"*. A folder just sums its children, and each child answers the same way, recursing down the tree. Add, remove, traverse, all uniform.\n\n> Watch the leaf-vs-composite asymmetry: `add()`/`remove()` only make sense on a composite. You either put them on the interface (and have leaves throw) or keep them composite-only, a real design trade-off. Composite is what lets recursion and the **Visitor** pattern walk a structure cleanly.",
