    "moduleId": "lld-behavioral",
    "subtitle": "Put the fixed skeleton of an algorithm in a base-class method, and let subclasses fill in the specific steps, witho…",
    "theoryMarkdown": "## The idea: a fixed skeleton, swappable steps\n\nSome algorithms are the **same shape every time**, even when the details differ. Mining records from a PDF, a CSV, or a database all follow one recipe: *open the source → extract raw data → parse into a common shape*. Only the **how** of each step changes; the **order never does**.\n\n**Template Method** captures exactly that split. A single base-class method — the *template method* — fixes the **algorithm skeleton**: the invariant steps and the order they run in. The steps that vary are deferred to subclasses. The structure lives in one place; the variation lives in another.\n\n## A real-world analogy: the beverage recipe\n\nThink of a coffee-shop machine making hot drinks. Every drink follows the same four steps:\n\n1. **boil water**\n2. **brew** the leaves or grounds\n3. **pour** into a cup\n4. **add condiments**\n\n*Boil* and *pour* are identical for tea and coffee — they belong in the base. *Brew* differs (steep tea bag vs. drip coffee grounds) and *condiments* differ (lemon vs. sugar + milk) — those belong in the subclasses. Nobody is allowed to pour before brewing: the **recipe order is locked**, only the variable steps are filled in per drink.\n\n## The mechanics: primitives and hooks\n\nThe template method is usually marked `final` so a subclass cannot reorder or replace the skeleton. It is built from two kinds of step:\n\n- **Primitive operations**, `abstract` methods the subclass **must** implement (`brew`, `extract`). These are the mandatory holes.\n- **Hooks**, methods with an **empty (or default) body** that a subclass **may** override but doesn't have to. They are optional extension points: an \"after parse\" callback, a \"should add condiments?\" toggle.\n\n```\nabstract class DataMiner {\n    final void mine() {        // the skeleton, locked\n        openFile();            // primitive (abstract)\n        extract();             // primitive (abstract)\n        parse();               // primitive (abstract)\n        summary();             // hook (optional override)\n    }\n    protected abstract void openFile();\n    protected abstract void extract();\n    protected abstract void parse();\n    protected void summary() {}   // default no-op hook\n}\n```\n\n## The Hollywood Principle\n\nWatch the direction of control. The client calls `mine()` on the base type, and the **base class calls DOWN** into the subclass's steps. The subclass never calls the framework; the framework calls the subclass. That inversion is the **Hollywood Principle: \"don't call us, we'll call you.\"** The base owns the flow; your code just plugs into the holes it leaves.\n\n## Template Method vs Strategy\n\nBoth let you vary behaviour, but along different axes:\n\n| | Template Method | Strategy |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mechanism | **Inheritance** (override steps) | **Composition** (swap an object) |\n| Bound at | **Compile time** (class hierarchy) | **Run time** (set a field) |\n| Granularity | Varies **a few steps** of one algorithm | Varies the **whole algorithm** |\n| Shared part | Parent holds the skeleton | Each strategy is self-contained |\n\n> **Takeaway:** reach for Template Method when the overall flow is fixed and only a few steps change; reach for Strategy when you need to swap an entire behaviour at runtime without subclassing.",
