    "moduleId": "lld-behavioral",
    "subtitle": "Walk through a collection's elements one by one WITHOUT exposing how the collection stores them, and support multip…",
    "theoryMarkdown": "## The idea: walk without looking inside\n\nYou want to visit every element of a collection, but you should not need to know *how* it stores them. A list keeps elements in a backing array, a tree threads them through left/right child links, a hash table scatters them across buckets. Three completely different layouts, yet the thing you want is identical every time: \"give me the next element until there are none left.\"\n\nThe **Iterator** pattern captures exactly that. It moves the job of *traversing* out of the collection and into a small separate object, the iterator, that knows how to step forward and report when it's done. The collection's only job becomes: \"hand out a fresh cursor when asked.\" It never has to expose its array, its nodes, or its buckets.\n\n## An analogy: the channel-up button\n\nThink of a TV remote's **channel-up** button. You press it and land on the *next* channel, then the next, then the next, without ever knowing whether the TV stores channels in a sorted list, a hash of frequencies, or a custom chip. The remote is your iterator; `channel-up` is `next()`; \"is there a channel after this one?\" is `hasNext()`. A museum **audio-guide** works the same way: press *next exhibit* and it steps you through the tour, while the layout of the building stays the guide's secret.\n\n## The mechanics: hasNext() and next()\n\nAn iterator is just two methods:\n\n```\ninterface Iterator {\n    boolean hasNext();   // is there another element?\n    Object  next();      // return it, then advance the cursor\n}\n```\n\nThe **cursor** (the current position) lives *inside the iterator*, never inside the collection. So the loop a client writes is always the same shape, no matter what's behind it:\n\n```\nIterator it = collection.iterator();\nwhile (it.hasNext()) {\n    process(it.next());\n}\n```\n\nSwap the array for a tree tomorrow and that loop does not change one character.\n\n## External vs internal iterators\n\nThere are two ways to drive the walk:\n\n- **External**, *you* hold the controls. You call `next()` yourself and decide what to do at each step. More verbose, but flexible: you can pause, break early, branch, or advance two iterators in lockstep (think *merge two sorted lists*).\n- **Internal**, *the collection* holds the controls. You hand it a function and it runs the loop for you: `coll.forEach(x -> process(x))`. Terser, but you give up fine-grained control over the stepping.\n\n## Why it pays off\n\n| Benefit | What it buys you |\n|---|---|\n| Uniform traversal | One `hasNext()`/`next()` loop walks an array, a tree, or a hash alike |\n| Encapsulation | The collection never leaks its internal structure to clients |\n| Multiple cursors | Each iterator owns its position, so many walks run at once, independently |\n| Lazy / infinite | `next()` can compute on demand, stream lines from a file, or yield forever |\n| Built into the language | Java `for(x:c)`, Python `for x in c`, C# `foreach` all desugar to this |\n\nThat third row is the quiet superpower: because the position lives in the iterator and not the collection, you can have **two independent cursors** crawling the same shelf at the same time and neither disturbs the other.\n\n## Fail-fast and concurrent modification\n\nOne sharp edge: what if the collection *changes* mid-walk? Most library iterators are **fail-fast**, they remember a modification count when created, and if you add or remove an element through the collection while iterating, the very next `next()` throws (Java's `ConcurrentModificationException`). It's a guard against silently skipping or double-visiting elements. To delete safely during a walk, use the iterator's own `remove()` instead of touching the collection directly.\n\n> The collection answers exactly one question: \"how do I make a fresh cursor?\", and is never told how it's being walked, how many walks are running, or when each one stops.",
