    "theory": "## When everyone talks to everyone\n\nA dialog box where the button enables a text field, the checkbox toggles a label, the field validates against another field… When many components must coordinate, wiring them **directly** creates an N² web: every component holds a reference to every other. Change one and you touch them all, it's impossible to follow, reuse, or test.\n\n## The pattern\n\nA **Mediator** centralizes the interaction. Each component knows only the mediator and notifies it of events, it never names another component. The mediator decides who reacts:\n\n```\nclass Dialog {            // the mediator\n    void notify(sender, ev){\n        if (sender == button && ev == \"click\")\n            textField.clear();    // mediator wires the reaction\n    }\n}\n```\n\nEach widget just calls `mediator.notify(this, ev)` and stays oblivious to the rest. Classic examples, a **dialog** coordinating its widgets, a **chat room** routing messages between users, **air-traffic control** sequencing planes that never talk to each other.\n\n## Trade-off\n\nThe relationships don't vanish, they move *into* the mediator. Keep an eye on it: a mediator that accumulates every rule can swell into a **god object**, a single class that knows too much. When that happens, split it.",
