    "moduleId": "lld-case-studies",
    "subtitle": "Group expense sharing: add an expense, split it (equally / exact / by %), and track who owes whom, then simplify th…",
    "theoryMarkdown": "## 1. Requirements\n\n- Users belong to **groups**; anyone can **add an expense** they paid for.\n- An expense splits among participants **equally**, by **exact amounts**, or by **percentage**.\n- Track a running **balance**, who owes whom and how much.\n- Bonus: **simplify debts** (if A owes B and B owes C, net it out).\n\n## 2. The classes\n\n`User`, `Group`, `Expense`, and a `BalanceSheet` that holds the net owed amounts. A `Group` **composes** its expenses (delete the group, the expenses go) and **aggregates** its members (users live independently).\n\n## 3. The key pattern: Strategy for splitting\n\nSplitting varies, equal, exact, percent, so it's a textbook **Strategy**. `SplitStrategy.split(amount, participants)` returns each person's share; `EqualSplit`, `ExactSplit`, `PercentSplit` implement it. Adding \"split by shares\" later is one new class, no edits.\n\n```\nList<Money> shares = expense.strategy.split(amount, participants);\n```\n\n## 4. Tracking balances\n\nThe `BalanceSheet` keeps a map like `owes[debtor][creditor] = amount`. When an expense is added, for each participant's share, record that they owe the payer that much (and net against anything the payer already owed them, so balances stay minimal).\n\n## 5. The hard bonus: simplify debts\n\nReducing a web of IOUs to the fewest transactions is a **graph problem**, compute each person's *net* balance, then match the biggest creditor with the biggest debtor repeatedly (a greedy heap). Mention it: it's the part that separates a good answer from a great one.",
